This project is for the GNU/Linux desktop.

This is just test C code, to figure out how to render fonts like for
example /usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSansMono.ttf

Yes, I know there are lots of solutions and examples; but we need
something that does not have leaky dependencies and is not bloated.  Both
GTK (libpango) and Qt are leaky and bloated.  Smaller FLTK is no longer
small because it now depends on GTK, which kind of defeats the whole point
of why FLTK exists.  I guess when X11 was killed, the FLTK developers did
not want to do the required hard work of making it work with Wayland, so
they just made it depend on GTK; and so it currently exists in a foo-bared
state.  Having this code depend on X11 is not an option; so that
eliminates lots of old GUI libraries.

Both libfontconfig and libfreetype seem to be small and robust. By far the
most common attribute of robust C code is that for all allocated memory
that a library creates, that memory can be freed by the user of that
library.  Both the GTK and the Qt libraries fail to meet this particular
attribute of robustness, both GTK and the Qt libraries have many objects
that do not have destructors; and since both GTK and the Qt are super
bloated, they are very hard to fix.

We will also strive to write unopinionated C code, kind of like
libfontconfig and libfreetype. 

1.  "It has been said that in writing unopinionated C code, the innate
flexibility can lead to a lack of consistency and potential complexity in
larger projects, making it harder to maintain and understand the
codebase"; ya, bullshit.  These libraries will be dependencies that are
not large to begin with, so that does not even make sense to say that.  If
you are writing a large complex code, it is that by you're doing.  There's
nothing large and complex here.  There is no innate reasons that flexible
software can't have reasonable defaults that can be overridden by the
user, libfontconfig has some good examples of that.

2.  "They are more adaptable but require more effort to set up and
maintain":  Not so, and quite the opposite.  I cry bullshit here too.
There is no general logic that shows adaptable software leads to require
more effort to set up and maintain; that is not generally so.  But,
there are examples of bad design.


